// Research Poster lessons 6–10: analyzing data, writing results, conclusions and the abstract,
// poster design, printing with upload and display time, and the Round Two presentation.
const RULES=['HOSA Research Poster 2026–27 guidelines and rating sheets','https://hosa.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Research-Poster-26-27.pdf'];
const STATS=['OpenStax Introductory Statistics 2e: Descriptive statistics','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/2-introduction'];
const STATS1=['OpenStax Introductory Statistics 2e: Sampling and data','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/1-introduction'];
const PURDUE=['Purdue OWL: Visual rhetoric and design','https://owl.purdue.edu/owl/general_writing/visual_rhetoric/index.html'];
const APA=['APA Style: References','https://apastyle.apa.org/style-grammar-guidelines/references'];
const UPLOAD=['HOSA: Competitive event digital uploads','https://hosa.org/competitive-event-digital-uploads/'];
const q=(p,a,b,c,d,e)=>[p,[a,b,c,d],e];
const match=(title,pairs)=>({t:'w',kind:'match',title,pairs});
const order=(title,steps)=>({t:'w',kind:'order',title,steps});
const sort=(title,buckets,items)=>({t:'w',kind:'sort',title,buckets,items});
const flip=(title,cards)=>({t:'w',kind:'flip',title,cards});
const kase=(title,nodes)=>({t:'w',kind:'case',title,start:'a',nodes});
const chk=(q,options,why)=>({t:'check',q,options,why});
const ex=(title,prompt,steps,answer)=>({t:'example',title,prompt,steps,answer});

export const RP6={title:'Analyzing quantitative and qualitative data',hook:'Raw data—stacks of surveys, tally sheets or interview notes—do not answer your question until you analyze them. The results section must describe both qualitative and quantitative results and present the data analysis employed. Simple, correct analysis is far better than complicated statistics you cannot explain.',objectives:['Organize and clean data for analysis.','Calculate and interpret percentages, means and medians.','Compare groups and describe relationships carefully.','Analyze qualitative data by identifying themes.'],sections:[
['Organizing and cleaning data',[
'Enter data into a **spreadsheet**, one row per participant and one column per question. Use **codes** for answers (for example, 1 = never, 5 = always). **Clean** the data: check for typing errors, impossible values (such as 30 hours of sleep), duplicates and incomplete responses, and decide consistently how to handle missing answers.',
'Keep an untouched copy of the original data and record every cleaning decision for your methods section.',
"Spreadsheet programs can calculate many statistics automatically, but always check a few results by hand to make sure formulas are correct and the right cells are included.",
order('Preparing data',['Enter data in a spreadsheet','Code responses consistently','Check for errors and impossible values','Remove duplicates','Decide how to handle missing answers','Save the original and cleaned versions'])]],
['Descriptive statistics',[
'**Descriptive statistics** summarize data. **Frequencies** and **percentages** show how many chose each answer. The **mean** (average) adds values and divides by the count. The **median** is the middle value when values are ordered—useful when a few extreme values would distort the mean. The **mode** is the most common value. The **range** shows the spread from lowest to highest.',
'Always report the **number of participants (n)** with percentages so readers know the base.',
"Round percentages consistently, usually to whole numbers or one decimal place, and note that totals may not equal exactly 100% because of rounding.",
ex('Mean and median','Seven students report sleep hours (invented for practice): 5, 6, 6, 7, 7, 8, 10. Find the mean and median.',['Sum: 5 + 6 + 6 + 7 + 7 + 8 + 10 = 49','Mean = 49 ÷ 7 = 7.0 hours','Ordered middle (4th) value = 7','Median = 7 hours; mode = 6 and 7 (two modes); range = 5 to 10'],'Mean 7.0 hours and median 7 hours (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Comparing groups',[
'To answer relationship questions, **compare groups**: for example, the percentage of high-stress students among those who sleep fewer than seven hours versus those who sleep eight or more. **Cross-tabulation** tables show two variables together.',
'Be careful with **small groups**—a difference based on very few people may not be meaningful. Simple comparisons with clear percentages are appropriate for most student research; if you use statistical tests, explain them simply.',
"Charts can make comparisons clearer than tables. A side-by-side bar chart showing two groups often communicates a relationship faster than several sentences of text.",
"When groups differ in size, compare percentages rather than raw counts. Forty students in one group and twenty in another cannot be compared fairly without converting to percentages.",
ex('Comparing percentages','Among 80 students sleeping under 7 hours, 44 report high stress; among 60 sleeping 8+ hours, 15 report high stress (invented for practice). Compare.',['Under 7 hours: 44 ÷ 80 = 55% high stress','8+ hours: 15 ÷ 60 = 25% high stress','Difference: 30 percentage points','Interpretation: high stress was more common among shorter sleepers'],'55% versus 25%—an association, not proof of cause (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Correlation versus causation',[
'A **correlation** or **association** means two variables tend to change together. It does **not** prove that one **causes** the other. Short sleep and stress might be related because stress causes poor sleep, poor sleep increases stress, or a third factor (such as heavy workloads) affects both.',
'Use careful language: “was associated with,” “was more common among” or “tended to,” rather than “caused.” Only well-designed experiments can support causal claims.',
"Look for possible third factors in your data. If students with jobs both sleep less and report more stress, the job may explain part of the relationship.",
sort('Careful or overstated claim?',['Careful','Overstated'],[['“Shorter sleep was associated with higher stress.”',0],['“Lack of sleep causes stress.”',1],['“High stress was more common among students who slept less.”',0],['“Our survey proves phones ruin sleep.”',1],['“Students who exercised tended to report better mood.”',0]])]],
['Analyzing qualitative data',[
'**Qualitative** data—interview notes, open-ended answers, observations—are analyzed by **coding**: reading responses, labeling ideas with short codes (such as “homework,” “phone use,” “sports”) and grouping codes into **themes**. Count how often each theme appears and choose **representative quotes**.',
'Qualitative findings explain the **why** behind numbers and make posters memorable.',
"Have two team members code a sample of responses separately and compare. Agreement between coders makes qualitative findings more reliable.",
"Report how many responses fit each theme, such as “homework was mentioned in 38 of 60 open-ended answers,” to show how common each idea was.",
match('Qualitative step and example',[['Read all responses','Review 60 open-ended answers'],['Code ideas','Label “phone use,” “homework,” “job”'],['Group into themes','“Academic pressure” theme'],['Count themes','Homework mentioned in 38 responses'],['Choose quotes','“I finish homework after midnight.”'],['Protect privacy','Remove names from quotes']])]],
['Checking your analysis',[
'Double-check calculations, confirm percentages add up correctly (within rounding) and make sure each result answers part of your **research question**. Have a teammate or advisor review the analysis.',
'Note **limitations** that affect interpretation, such as a low response rate or a sample from one school. These belong in the methods and conclusions.',
"Record your analysis steps in the research log so the methods section can explain exactly how data were analyzed and so judges’ questions are easy to answer.",
chk('Your percentages for one question add to 112%. What does this suggest?',['A calculation or data-entry error—or a “select all that apply” question that should be explained','Nothing; it is normal for all questions','The survey was perfect','The sample size doubled'],'Check calculations and question types.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Analysis decisions',{a:{text:'One survey reports 30 hours of sleep per night. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Treat it as an impossible value, exclude it consistently and note this in methods','b','Correct.',true],['Include it in the mean','b','Impossible values distort results.',false]]},b:{text:'Your data show shorter sleepers report more stress. How do you write it?',choices:[['“Shorter sleep was associated with higher reported stress.”','c','Correct.',true],['“Short sleep causes stress.”','c','A survey shows association, not causation.',false]]},c:{text:'Your analysis is accurate and carefully worded.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Enter data in a spreadsheet with consistent codes; clean errors, impossible values, duplicates and missing answers; keep the original and record decisions.','Use frequencies, percentages (with n), mean, median, mode and range; compare groups with cross-tabulations and percentages; be cautious with small groups.','Association is not causation—use careful language; code qualitative data into themes with representative, de-identified quotes; double-check calculations and note limitations.'],terms:[['Median','The middle value when data are ordered.'],['Correlation','A relationship in which two variables tend to change together.'],['Coding','Labeling qualitative responses to identify themes.']],mcqs:[q('Which measure is least affected by extreme values?','Median','Mean','Range','Sum','Robust.'),q('The mean of 4, 6 and 8 is:','6','4','8','18','Average.'),q('What should you report with percentages?','The number of participants (n)','Participants’ names','Nothing else','The judges’ names','Context.'),q('A correlation shows:','Variables tend to change together','One variable causes another','No relationship','The research question','Definition.'),q('Which is careful wording?','“Was associated with”','“Proves that”','“Causes”','“Always leads to”','Language.'),q('Qualitative analysis groups codes into:','Themes','Means','Ranges','Percentiles','Coding.'),q('If 18 of 60 students report daily exercise, what percentage is that?','30%','18%','60%','42%','18 ÷ 60.'),q('What should you do with an impossible value such as 30 hours of sleep?','Exclude it consistently and note it','Include it','Change it to 8','Ignore all data','Cleaning.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would analyze quantitative and qualitative data from a student health survey.',rubric:[['Preparation',['spreadsheet','code','clean','errors','missing','original']],['Descriptive statistics',['percentages','mean','median','mode','range','n']],['Comparisons',['compare','groups','cross-tabulation','association','causation','careful']],['Qualitative',['coding','themes','quotes','count','privacy','limitations']]],model:'After collecting 172 anonymous surveys on sleep and stress, we would enter each response as a row in a spreadsheet, coding answers consistently, such as stress ratings from 1 to 5. We would clean the data by checking for typing errors, excluding impossible values such as 30 hours of sleep, removing duplicates and handling missing answers consistently, keeping an untouched copy of the original and recording every decision for our methods section. Next, we would calculate descriptive statistics: the percentage of students sleeping fewer than eight hours, always reporting the number of participants, plus the mean and median sleep hours, since the median is less affected by extreme values, and the range. To answer our relationship question, we would create a cross-tabulation comparing the percentage of students reporting high stress among those sleeping fewer than seven hours versus those sleeping eight or more—for example, 55% versus 25%—and describe this carefully as an association, not proof that short sleep causes stress, noting possible third factors like homework load. For open-ended answers about barriers to sleep, we would read all responses, code ideas such as “homework,” “phone use” and “jobs,” group them into themes, count how often each appears and select representative quotes with no identifying details. Finally, we would double-check calculations with a teammate and our advisor and note limitations such as our response rate and single-school sample.'},sources:[RULES,STATS]};

export const RP7={title:'Writing the abstract, methods, results and conclusions',hook:'Poster text must be concise, accurate and persuasive—each section has a suggested word limit, and judges score the abstract, methods, results and conclusions at 10 points each. Clear scientific writing lets observers understand your research without you present. This lesson covers what each section must include and how to write it well.',objectives:['Write each poster section to the guideline requirements.','Stay within suggested word limits.','Use active, third-person, concise scientific language.','Write a strong title and acknowledgements.'],sections:[
['The title',[
'The **title** should highlight the research and grab attention, and the title area must include **competitors’ names**, **HOSA division**, **chapter number**, **school name** and **chartered association**—**100 words maximum** in total.',
'Strong titles are specific and informative, sometimes with a short hook: “Sleepless and Stressed? Sleep Duration and Stress Among Students at Lincoln High School.” Avoid vague titles such as “Sleep Project.”',
'The title item is worth 5 points.',
"Make the title specific enough that a reader knows the topic, population and setting at a glance. A short question or phrase before a colon can add interest without losing clarity.",
"Place the team details in a smaller font beneath the title so the research title remains the most prominent text on the poster.",
sort('Strong or weak title?',['Strong','Weak'],[['“Sleepless and Stressed? Sleep and Stress Among Lincoln High Students”',0],['“Our Project”',1],['“Knowledge of Safe Medication Disposal Among Local Parents”',0],['“Health Stuff”',1],['“Hand Hygiene Before Lunch in Two Middle School Cafeterias”',0]])]],
['The abstract',[
'The **abstract** (about **250 words**) is a brief summary of the whole study. Include the **overall purpose** and **research problem**, the **basic design and objectives**, the **significant findings** from your analysis and a **brief summary of interpretations and conclusions**.',
'Write the abstract **last**, after the other sections are complete. It should stand on its own—someone reading only the abstract should understand your study and its main findings.',
"Include at least one key number in the abstract’s findings. A specific result, such as a percentage, is more convincing than a general statement.",
"Draft the abstract early as a planning tool, then rewrite it at the end with final results. The early draft keeps the team focused; the final version must match the data.",
order('Abstract structure',['Problem and purpose','Research question','Design and methods','Key findings with numbers','Interpretation and conclusion'])]],
['Methods and results',[
'**Methods** (about **200 words**): describe the research methods, **target population**, how data were **collected accurately**, how data were **analyzed** and **possible errors and biases**. Write so another team could repeat your study.',
'**Results** (about **200 words**): describe **qualitative and quantitative** results, present the **data analysis** used, explain **why the results matter** and use **supportive charts and figures**. Report findings objectively without interpreting too much—save interpretation for the conclusions.',
"Results should match the charts exactly. If the text says 68% but the chart shows 67%, judges will notice the inconsistency, so check every number before printing.",
"Keep methods and results separate. Describing what you found belongs in results, while explaining what it means belongs in conclusions—mixing them makes sections harder to follow.",
match('Section and required content',[['Methods','Target population and data collection'],['Methods','Analysis and possible biases'],['Results','Quantitative and qualitative findings'],['Results','Charts and figures'],['Results','Why the results matter'],['Abstract','Purpose, design, findings and conclusions']])]],
['Conclusions',[
'**Conclusions** (about **200 words**) **emphasize the significant results**, try to **convince readers why they are interesting** and explain their **relevance to your community and the world**. Answer your research question directly, compare with background research, acknowledge key limitations and suggest **recommendations** or **next steps**, such as a school sleep campaign or later start times.',
'Avoid overstating: base claims on your evidence.',
"Recommendations should follow directly from your findings. If homework timing was the most common barrier to sleep, a recommendation about homework scheduling is well supported.",
"End the conclusions with a forward-looking sentence, such as a recommended action or future study, so readers leave with a clear sense of why the research matters.",
ex('A conclusion paragraph','Draft key conclusion sentences (findings invented for practice).',['Answer: “Most students (68%) slept fewer than eight hours on school nights.”','Significance: “High stress was more than twice as common among students sleeping under seven hours.”','Comparison: “This is similar to national survey findings.”','Relevance and next steps: “Our school could promote sleep education and review homework scheduling.”','Limitation: “Results come from one school and self-reported data.”'],'A focused, evidence-based conclusion (findings invented for practice).')]],
['Scientific writing style',[
'The guidelines suggest **active** language, a **third-person** point of view, **bullet points** with brief statements, **left-justified** text and **text boxes** for easier layout. Use precise numbers, define terms and avoid filler words.',
'Edit ruthlessly to meet word limits: cut repetition, replace phrases with single words and move details to charts. Proofread every section—spelling and grammar affect the appearance and organization score.',
"Read each section aloud. Sentences that are hard to say aloud are usually hard to read on a poster, so shorten or simplify them.",
"Have someone outside the team read each section and summarize it in one sentence. If their summary differs from your intended message, revise for clarity.",
sort('Good poster writing?',['Good','Poor'],[['“68% of students slept under 8 hours (n = 172).”',0],['“It was found by us that many people kind of slept less.”',1],['Bullet points with brief statements',0],['Long paragraphs in all capital letters',1],['Third-person, active voice',0]])]],
['References and acknowledgements',[
'**References** list literature that guided the project in **APA** style (about 100 words). **Acknowledgements** thank anyone who helped make the project possible—your advisor, school administration, participants, community partners or professionals who reviewed your work. At least one person or organization should be acknowledged (5 points).',
'Logos of community agencies involved in the research are acceptable as images.',
"Acknowledgements are a good place to thank participants as a group, such as “the students of Lincoln High School,” without naming individuals.",
chk('Who should be acknowledged?',['People and organizations who helped make the project possible','No one','Only the judges','Only celebrities'],'Thank those who helped.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Writing review',{a:{text:'Your methods section is 450 words. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Edit to about 200 words using bullet points and concise phrasing','b','Correct.',true],['Shrink the font','b','Readability matters; edit the text.',false]]},b:{text:'Your conclusion says, “Our study proves that phones cause stress.” How do you revise it?',choices:[['“Phone use at night was associated with higher reported stress.”','c','Correct.',true],['Keep it—it sounds strong','c','Avoid overstated causal claims.',false]]},c:{text:'Your sections are concise, accurate and within limits.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Title (≤100 words with names, division, chapter number, school, chartered association) should be specific and attention-grabbing; the abstract (about 250 words) covers purpose, design, findings and conclusions and is written last.','Methods (about 200 words): methods, target population, accurate collection, analysis and biases; results (about 200 words): qualitative and quantitative findings, analysis, why they matter and charts; conclusions (about 200 words): significant results, relevance, comparisons, limitations and next steps.','Write in active, third-person, concise bullets, left-justified in text boxes; proofread; list APA references (about 100 words) and thank helpers in acknowledgements.'],terms:[['Abstract','A brief summary of a study’s purpose, methods, findings and conclusions.'],['Results','The section that presents findings objectively.'],['Conclusions','The section that interprets results and explains their significance.']],mcqs:[q('About how many words are suggested for the abstract?','250','100','500','50','Limit.'),q('What is the maximum for the title area?','100 words','250 words','10 words','500 words','Limit.'),q('Which section explains possible errors and biases?','Methods','Title','Acknowledgements','References','Requirement.'),q('When should the abstract usually be written?','Last','First','Never','Before choosing a question','Practice.'),q('Which belongs in the results section?','Charts and figures of findings','Thank-you notes','The research question only','The team’s names','Content.'),q('What point of view is suggested?','Third person','First person only','Second person','No point of view','Style.'),q('Which conclusion sentence is best?','“Phone use at night was associated with higher stress.”','“Phones cause stress.”','“We proved everything.”','“It was good.”','Careful claims.'),q('What do acknowledgements do?','Thank those who helped','List statistics','Replace references','State the hypothesis','Purpose.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe what your team would write in each section of the poster and how you would keep the writing clear and concise.',rubric:[['Title and abstract',['title','names','division','chapter','abstract','purpose','findings','last']],['Methods and results',['methods','population','collected','analyzed','bias','results','charts']],['Conclusions',['significant','relevance','community','limitations','next steps','compare']],['Style and extras',['active','third person','bullet','word limits','proofread','apa','acknowledgements']]],model:'Our title area would read “Sleepless and Stressed? Sleep Duration and Stress Among Students at Lincoln High School,” followed by our names, division, chapter number, school and chartered association, all within 100 words. We would write the abstract last, in about 250 words, summarizing the problem of insufficient teen sleep, our question about sleep and stress, our anonymous survey of 172 randomly selected students, key findings with numbers and our main conclusion. The methods section, about 200 words, would describe our target population of all students, random homeroom sampling, the pilot-tested survey and consent procedures, how we cleaned and analyzed data with percentages, means and cross-tabulations and themes from open-ended answers, and possible selection, response and recall biases. The results section, about 200 words, would present quantitative findings—such as 68% sleeping fewer than eight hours and high stress being 55% among short sleepers versus 25% among those sleeping eight or more—along with qualitative themes like homework and phone use, two charts and why the results matter. Our conclusions, about 200 words, would emphasize the most significant result, compare it with national data, explain its relevance to our school, acknowledge that it shows association from one school’s self-reported data and recommend sleep education and a review of homework scheduling. Throughout, we would write in active, third-person bullet points, left-justified in text boxes, edit to the word limits, proofread carefully, list our key APA references in about 100 words and thank our advisor, principal and participants in the acknowledgements.'},sources:[RULES,APA]};

export const RP8={title:'Designing the poster',hook:'Judges award 20 points for artistic design and appearance and organization, and 10 more for images. At ILC, a strong design draws visitors from across the room and guides them through your research in seconds. The guidelines offer practical design tips—from the three-foot rule to image resolution—that turn good research into an impressive poster.',objectives:['Lay out the poster for a logical reading flow.','Apply font size and readability guidelines.','Choose and prepare effective images and charts.','Use color, contrast and white space effectively.'],sections:[
['Size and template',[
'Create the poster **digitally** (not hand-drawn) in any program that can save a **PDF**, sized at **48″ × 36″ in landscape orientation**. Set the document size before you begin so text and images are scaled correctly. Colors, fonts and overall design are your team’s choice, but all eight components must be included.',
'Many websites show sample poster templates and designs, which can help you see strengths and weaknesses—but your design and content must be your own.',
'Poster size is scored in Round Two (5 points).',
"Start the design with a rough sketch on paper showing where each section and image will go. Sketching first saves time compared with rearranging boxes on screen repeatedly.",
chk('How should the poster be created?',['Digitally, 48″ × 36″ landscape, saved as a PDF','Hand-drawn on poster board','Any size in portrait orientation','As a slideshow'],'Template rules.')]],
['Layout and reading flow',[
'Most readers read in **columns**: top left to bottom, then the next column. Arrange sections in order—**title** across the top, then **abstract** and **methods** on the left, **results** in the center (often the largest area with charts) and **conclusions**, **references** and **acknowledgements** on the right.',
'Use **text boxes** for each section, align them carefully and leave **white space** between sections. Consistent spacing and alignment make the poster look organized and professional.',
"Make the results section the visual focus, since it contains your most important findings. A large central chart draws viewers in and leads them to the conclusions.",
"Use consistent margins around the edge of the poster so nothing is cut off during printing. Many printers recommend keeping important content at least an inch from the edges.",
order('Typical three-column layout',['Title banner across the top','Left column: abstract and methods','Center column: results with charts','Right column: conclusions','Bottom right: references and acknowledgements'])]],
['The three-foot rule and fonts',[
'The poster should be **readable from 3 feet away**. Suggested sizes: **title at least 65 pt**, **headings at least 48 pt** and **all other text at least 24 pt** (36–42 pt suggested). Captions should be **italicized** and at least **18 pt**.',
'Use **bold** for emphasis, but avoid **underlining** and **ALL CAPITALS**, which are harder to read. Choose one or two clean, readable fonts and use them consistently.',
"Print a small test section at full size to check font readability. What looks large on a computer screen can appear smaller when printed on a 48-inch poster.",
"Limit text so the poster is not crowded. Many successful posters use far fewer words than the suggested maximums, letting charts and images carry key information.",
match('Text element and suggested minimum',[['Title','65 pt'],['Headings','48 pt'],['Body text','24 pt (36–42 pt suggested)'],['Captions','18 pt, italicized'],['Emphasis','Bold, not underline or capitals'],['Fonts','One or two consistent families']])]],
['Images and charts',[
'Include **2 to 5 images**: graphs, tables, statistics or quotes illustrating findings, plus photos or illustrations that reflect the research. Logos from community agencies involved are acceptable. Choose the right chart: **bar charts** compare groups, **pie charts** show parts of a whole (use sparingly), **line graphs** show change over time.',
'Use **high-quality images**—photos print best at **300 dpi or greater** (TIFF format is often recommended)—and avoid grainy or distorted pictures. Avoid **long numeric tables**; convert data into clear charts with labeled axes and titles. Add italicized captions.',
"Label every chart with a title, axis labels and units, and include the sample size. A chart should make sense even if someone looks at it without reading the text.",
"If you include photos of participants or locations, confirm you have permission and avoid identifiable images of minors unless consent policies allow it.",
sort('Good image choice?',['Good','Poor'],[['A labeled bar chart comparing stress by sleep group',0],['A blurry photo copied from the web',1],['A short quote from an interview participant',0],['A 40-row table of raw survey data',1],['A high-resolution photo of the observation site (with permission)',0]])]],
['Color, contrast and background',[
'Use **high-contrast** colors on **muted backgrounds**, such as dark text on a white or light background. Avoid **dark backgrounds** and **patterns**, which are costly to print and harder to read.',
'Pick a limited **color scheme**—two or three colors—that reflects your topic, and use color consistently for headings and chart elements. Check that colors are distinguishable for people with color vision deficiencies.',
'Design should support understanding, not distract from it.',
"Use the same color for the same group in every chart—for example, teal for short sleepers and orange for longer sleepers—so viewers can follow comparisons easily.",
ex('Checking contrast and color','Review a draft poster’s colors.',['Background: changed from navy pattern to off-white','Headings: dark teal for all sections','Charts: teal and orange (distinguishable for most color-blind viewers)','Body text: near-black on off-white'],'High contrast, a limited palette and lower printing costs.')]],
['Self-contained and organized',[
'The best posters are **self-contained and self-explanatory**—observers should understand the research without the team present. Ask someone unfamiliar with the project to read the poster for three minutes and explain the question, methods, main finding and conclusion.',
'The **appearance and organization** item rewards a neat, logical, error-free poster. Proofread every word, check alignment and confirm all eight components are present.',
"Zoom out on screen to view the whole poster at once. This reveals uneven spacing, crowded areas and sections that do not stand out.",
order('Final design review',['Confirm all eight components are present','Check font sizes against the three-foot rule','Check images are high resolution with captions','Proofread all text','Ask an outside reader to explain the poster','Revise and export as a PDF'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Design review',{a:{text:'Your body text is 16 pt to fit all your words. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you do?',choices:[['Cut words and raise body text to at least 24 pt','b','Correct.',true],['Keep it—more text is better','b','The poster must be readable from 3 feet.',false]]},b:{text:'You want a black background with neon text. What does the guidance suggest?',choices:[['Use a muted background with high-contrast text to improve readability and printing','c','Correct.',true],['Dark backgrounds are recommended','c','Avoid dark backgrounds and patterns.',false]]},c:{text:'Your poster is readable, organized and attractive.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Design digitally at 48″ × 36″ landscape and save as a PDF; arrange sections in reading order (columns, top-left to bottom) with text boxes, alignment and white space.','Three-foot rule: title ≥65 pt, headings ≥48 pt, other text ≥24 pt (36–42 suggested), italic captions ≥18 pt; use bold, not underlining or capitals.','Include 2–5 high-quality images (≥300 dpi), clear labeled charts instead of long tables, high-contrast text on muted backgrounds and a limited color scheme; make the poster self-explanatory and error-free.'],terms:[['Three-foot rule','The guideline that a poster should be readable from three feet away.'],['White space','Empty space that separates sections and improves readability.'],['Resolution','Image detail, measured in dots per inch (dpi).']],mcqs:[q('What is the suggested minimum title font size?','65 pt','24 pt','12 pt','100 pt','Guideline.'),q('What is the suggested minimum heading size?','48 pt','18 pt','24 pt','72 pt','Guideline.'),q('What is the minimum suggested size for other text?','24 pt','12 pt','10 pt','48 pt','Guideline.'),q('Photos print best at:','300 dpi or greater','72 dpi','10 dpi','Any resolution','Quality.'),q('Which background is recommended?','A muted, light background','A dark patterned background','Neon colors','A photo collage','Printing and readability.'),q('How many images should the poster include?','2 to 5','None','10 or more','Exactly 1','Requirement.'),q('Which is best for comparing groups?','A bar chart','A long numeric table','A paragraph','A pie chart of unrelated data','Chart choice.'),q('Which emphasis is recommended?','Bold','Underlining','ALL CAPITALS','Blinking text','Style.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would design a research poster that scores well for artistic design, organization and images.',rubric:[['Template and layout',['48','36','landscape','pdf','columns','text boxes','white space']],['Readability',['three-foot','65','48','24','18','bold','capitals']],['Images',['2 to 5','charts','bar','300 dpi','captions','tables']],['Color and review',['contrast','muted','background','palette','self-explanatory','proofread']]],model:'We would design our poster digitally in a program that exports to PDF, setting the document to 48 by 36 inches in landscape orientation before adding anything. Our layout would follow a three-column reading flow: a title banner across the top with our names, division, chapter number, school and chartered association; the abstract and methods in the left column; results in the larger center column with two charts; and conclusions, references and acknowledgements on the right, each in aligned text boxes with consistent white space. Following the three-foot rule, our title would be at least 65 points, headings at least 48 points and body text about 36 points, with italicized captions of at least 18 points, using bold for emphasis instead of underlining or capital letters and one clean font family. We would include four images: a labeled bar chart comparing high stress among students sleeping under seven hours versus eight or more, a chart of sleep-hour distribution, a short interview quote in a highlighted box and a high-resolution photo of our survey table taken with permission, all at 300 dpi or greater, avoiding long numeric tables. For color, we would use near-black text on an off-white background with dark teal headings and teal and orange chart colors that remain distinguishable for most color-blind viewers, avoiding dark or patterned backgrounds to improve readability and reduce printing costs. Finally, we would confirm all eight components are present, proofread every word and ask a teacher unfamiliar with our project to explain the poster after three minutes, revising anything unclear.'},sources:[RULES,PURDUE]};

export const RP9={title:'Printing, uploading and the display time',hook:'Your research is done and your poster is designed—now it has to reach the judges and the conference. One PDF goes online by May 15 for pre-judging, and a printed 48-by-36-inch poster goes to ILC for the required display time and Round Two. Careful logistics protect all the points your research has earned.',objectives:['Upload a correct, single PDF by the deadline.','Print the poster affordably at the correct size.','Prepare for the Required Project Display Time.','Share research confidently with conference visitors.'],sections:[
['The digital upload',[
'One team member uploads the **research poster as one PDF file** to the HOSA Digital Upload System by **May 15 at midnight EST**—**no exceptions**. If it is not a single PDF, judges will not view it and no points are awarded, and inaccessible links or documents earn no points.',
'Before uploading, open the PDF to confirm all text, images and charts display correctly at full size. Upload early to avoid system problems. If your state uses the HOSA upload system for its conference, upload **again** for ILC.',
'Teams that do not upload are not eligible for Round Two.',
"Name the PDF clearly, such as with the event and team or school name, so it is easy to identify. Check the file size so it uploads without problems.",
order('Uploading the poster',['Export the final poster as one PDF','Open and check every element at full zoom','Confirm the file name identifies the team','Upload through the HOSA Digital Upload System','Confirm the upload was received','Save a backup copy'])]],
['Pre-judging and advancement',[
'Events with a required display time are **pre-judged in early June**, and Round Two finalists are determined right after. **Results are not released until after the display time**, so all teams attend display time without knowing whether they advanced. Some teams may participate only in the display time.',
'Judges’ decisions are final. The number of advancing teams depends on Round One scores and available space and time.',
"Because results are released after display time, prepare for Round Two at the same time as display time. Have notecards and roles ready in case your team advances.",
chk('When are Round Two finalists announced?',['After the Required Display Time at ILC','Before teams upload','On May 15','Never'],'Results follow display time.')]],
['Printing the poster',[
'Print the final poster at **48″ × 36″ landscape**. To reduce cost, posters may be printed on **matte or economy paper**—glossy paper is not required—and on **thin paper that rolls up easily**; **foam boards are not needed**. Any material works as long as a **push pin** can attach it to the provided standing bulletin board.',
'Ask your advisor about school, college or university printing departments with discounted rates, or use affordable online printing services. Order early, check a proof and transport the poster in a tube to avoid creases.',
"Order the print at least two weeks before you travel. Delivery delays and printing errors are common, and a reprint may be needed.",
sort('Recommended printing choice?',['Recommended','Not needed or not recommended'],[['Matte economy paper',0],['Heavy foam board mounting',1],['Rolled thin paper in a poster tube',0],['Dark patterned background that uses lots of ink',1],['Ordering early and checking a proof',0]])]],
['The Required Project Display Time',[
'All competitors must attend the **Required Project Display Time** at ILC, standing with their posters and sharing research with delegates. Missing it costs **15 points** from Round Two. Teams have **five minutes** to attach their poster to the provided standing bulletin board, and HOSA provides **four push pins**. Only registered competitors may set up.',
'Setup methods may vary—walls, tables or other methods. Pick up the poster as instructed; unclaimed displays become HOSA property and may be discarded.',
"Practice attaching the poster with four pins at home on a bulletin board or wall. Knowing where to place pins helps you set up smoothly within five minutes.",
"Bring a few small printed handouts with your abstract or a QR code to the PDF if allowed, so interested visitors can learn more after display time.",
flip('Display time facts',[['Attendance','Required for all teams'],['Penalty','−15 points from Round Two'],['Setup time','5 minutes'],['Push pins','Four, provided by HOSA'],['Setup by','Registered competitors only'],['Pickup','As instructed, or it may be discarded']])]],
['Talking with visitors',[
'During display time, delegates, judges and other visitors may ask about your research. Prepare a **30-second summary**: the question, how you studied it, the main finding and why it matters. Invite questions and point to charts as you explain.',
'Be ready for common questions: How did you choose your topic? How many people participated? What surprised you? What would you do next? Each conversation is practice for Round Two.',
"Take turns speaking with visitors so every member practices explaining the research. Rotating also keeps the team fresh during a long display session.",
ex('A 30-second summary','Draft a display-time summary (findings invented for practice).',['Question: “We asked whether sleep is linked to stress at our school.”','Method: “We surveyed 172 randomly selected students.”','Finding: “High stress was more than twice as common among students sleeping under seven hours.”','Why it matters: “It suggests sleep education could help students manage stress.”'],'A clear summary that invites follow-up questions (findings invented for practice).')]],
['Packing and backup plans',[
'Pack the poster in a sturdy tube, bring extra push pins or clips, tape and a small printed copy for reference. Keep the PDF on a phone or laptop in case the poster is damaged and needs reprinting nearby. Dress professionally for display time and Round Two.',
'Confirm the display time and Round Two schedule in the conference program.',
"Photograph your poster at the display for your chapter records and future reference. Photos also help next year’s team learn from your design.",
order('Before leaving for ILC',['Inspect the printed poster for errors','Roll it into a sturdy tube','Pack extra pins, clips and tape','Save the PDF on a device','Print a small reference copy','Confirm the schedule'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Logistics',{a:{text:'Your teammate uploads the poster as six separate image files. (Scenario invented for practice.) What happens?',choices:[['Judges will not view it—upload a single PDF before the deadline','b','Correct.',true],['Judges will combine them','b','Only one PDF is accepted.',false]]},b:{text:'At ILC, a teammate wants to skip display time because results are not out yet. Should they?',choices:[['No—all competitors must attend, or the team loses 15 points','c','Correct.',true],['Yes—display time is optional','c','Display time is required.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team uploads correctly and attends display time.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Upload one PDF by May 15 at midnight EST (no exceptions); check it at full zoom; upload again for ILC if your state used the system; teams that do not upload cannot compete in Round Two.','Pre-judging happens in early June; results are released after the display time; print at 48″ × 36″ landscape on matte or thin rollable paper (no foam board needed) and order early.','Display time is required (−15 if missed), with 5 minutes to attach the poster using four HOSA push pins; prepare a 30-second summary, pack carefully, keep a PDF backup and pick up the poster as instructed.'],terms:[['Pre-judging','Scoring of uploaded materials before the conference.'],['Display time','A required ILC session where teams share their posters.'],['Proof','A preview copy checked before final printing.']],mcqs:[q('How must the poster be uploaded?','As one PDF file','As separate images','As a link to a website','On paper','Rule.'),q('When is the upload deadline?','May 15 at midnight EST','June 1','The day of ILC','January 1','Deadline.'),q('When are Round Two finalists announced?','After the Required Display Time','Before upload','On May 15','At registration','Timing.'),q('Is foam board mounting required?','No','Yes','Only for postsecondary','Only for winners','Printing.'),q('How many push pins does HOSA provide?','Four','Two','Ten','None','Setup.'),q('How long do teams have to attach their posters?','5 minutes','30 minutes','1 hour','1 minute','Setup.'),q('What is the penalty for missing display time?','15 points from Round Two','5 points','Disqualification','None','Rule.'),q('Which paper is acceptable and economical?','Matte economy paper','Only high-gloss paper','Metal sheets','Canvas only','Cost.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the steps your team would take to upload, print and display the research poster successfully.',rubric:[['Upload',['one pdf','may 15','check','upload again','not eligible']],['Pre-judging',['early june','after display time','finalists','space']],['Printing',['48','36','landscape','matte','thin','push pin','order early']],['Display time',['required','15 points','5 minutes','four push pins','summary','pickup']]],model:'When our poster is finished, we would export it as a single PDF, open it at full zoom to check every chart, image and word and have one member upload it to the HOSA Digital Upload System well before May 15 at midnight EST, confirming the upload and saving a backup; if our state conference used the system, we would upload again for ILC. We know that pre-judging happens in early June and that finalists are not announced until after the Required Display Time, so every member would plan to attend. For printing, we would ask our advisor about the school district’s print shop or a university printing department with discounted rates, ordering early in May at 48 by 36 inches in landscape orientation on matte economy paper that rolls up easily, since glossy paper and foam boards are not required, and we would check a proof for errors. We would transport the poster in a sturdy tube with extra pins, clips and tape, keep the PDF on a laptop in case it needed reprinting and bring a small reference copy. At ILC, we would arrive on time for display time, use our five minutes and HOSA’s four push pins to attach the poster to the standing bulletin board and stand with it to share our research, using a 30-second summary of our question, methods, main finding and why it matters, while answering visitors’ questions. Attending display time protects us from the 15-point penalty, and afterward we would pick up the poster as instructed.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOAD]};

export const RP10={title:'The Round Two presentation',hook:'Standing beside your poster, your team has five minutes to bring your research to life. Judges score your opening, your presentation of the research and your connection to the poster at 15 points each—45 of the 70 Round Two points. The best presentations start with an energetic elevator pitch, highlight key findings and complement the poster rather than reading it.',objectives:['Open with a compelling elevator pitch.','Present the research’s key findings and implications.','Connect the presentation to the poster without duplicating it.','Deliver as a coordinated team within five minutes.'],sections:[
['Round Two requirements',[
'Teams stand **next to their poster** and give a **five-minute prepared presentation**, with a time card at **one minute remaining**. Notecards are allowed (electronic notes are not shown). **All team members must take an active role**.',
'Content requirements: **(a)** begin with an **elevator pitch**—a short, enthusiastic introduction that draws judges in and shows why the research is essential; **(b)** stay **clearly connected to the poster** but **do not simply duplicate it**; and **(c)** **highlight the salient points**—key findings and implications.',
'Round Two also scores poster size (5 points), so bring the correctly printed 48″ × 36″ poster.',
"Practice the presentation beside a full-size poster or a printed copy taped to a wall, so movements and gestures feel natural on competition day.",
"Practice transitions such as “Now my teammate will explain what we found,” so each member hands off smoothly and judges can follow the structure.",
flip('Round Two scoring',[['Opening (elevator pitch)','15'],['Presentation of the research','15'],['Connection to the poster','15'],['Voice, stage presence, diction','5 each'],['Team participation','5'],['Poster size','5']])]],
['The elevator pitch',[
'An **elevator pitch** is a short, enthusiastic opening—about 20–40 seconds—that answers: What did we study? Why does it matter? What did we find that is surprising or important? Start with a **hook**: a striking statistic from your data, a question or a short story.',
'Example: “Two out of three students at our school sleep less than eight hours on school nights—and those getting the least sleep were twice as likely to report high stress. Today we’ll show you what we found and what our school can do about it.”',
'A strong opening earns up to 15 points and sets the tone for everything after.',
"Use your most surprising or meaningful finding in the elevator pitch. Judges hear many presentations, and a memorable number or story helps yours stand out.",
sort('Strong or weak opening?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Starting with a striking local statistic and why it matters',0],['“Um, so, our poster is about sleep.”',1],['A short question that connects to judges’ experience',0],['Reading the title aloud word for word',1],['Previewing the key finding and its importance',0]])]],
['Presenting the research',[
'Walk judges through the research story: the **problem and question**, **why you chose it**, **how you studied it** (briefly), **key findings**, **implications** and **next steps**. Focus on the **most important** findings rather than every number.',
'Explain your reasoning and decisions—why you chose your methods, how you reduced bias and what surprised you. Show deep understanding by explaining limitations and what you would do differently.',
"Show judges what you learned as researchers, not just what you found. Explaining a challenge you overcame, such as low response in one grade, demonstrates real research skill.",
"Connect your findings to people. Explaining how results could affect students, families or health workers makes the research meaningful and memorable for judges.",
order('Research story',['Problem and question','Why it matters locally','How we studied it','Key findings','Implications for our community','Limitations and next steps'])]],
['Connecting to the poster',[
'Use the poster as a **visual guide**: point to charts as you explain results, gesture toward the methods section when describing your survey and refer to quotes. But **do not read the poster**—add context, stories and insights that complement it, such as how participants reacted or challenges you overcame.',
'Stand to the side so judges can see the poster, and move naturally between sections as each member speaks.',
"Decide in advance who will point to each part of the poster. Coordinated movement looks professional and prevents members from crowding the same area.",
chk('Which best shows connection without duplication?',['Pointing to the bar chart while explaining what surprised the team about it','Reading the results section word for word','Ignoring the poster entirely','Standing in front of the chart'],'Complement, not copy.')]],
['Structuring five minutes',[
'A sample plan: **Elevator pitch (about 30 seconds)**; **problem, question and methods (about 1 minute)**; **key findings with charts (about 1½ minutes)**; **conclusions, implications and next steps (about 1 minute)**; **closing (about 30 seconds)**. Rehearse to about **4 minutes 45 seconds**.',
'Divide sections so each member speaks equally, with smooth handoffs.',
"Leave a few seconds at the end for a strong closing line. Ending with your recommendation or a call to action leaves judges with a clear final impression.",
"Avoid reading notecards. Use them only for key phrases and numbers, keeping your eyes on the judges and the poster most of the time.",
ex('Timing plan for three members','Plan a five-minute presentation (one possible split).',['Member 1: elevator pitch and question (1:00)','Member 2: methods and key findings (1:45)','Member 3: conclusions, implications and next steps (1:30)','Member 1: closing line (0:30)','Total: 4:45'],'Every member speaks, with a 15-second margin.')]],
['Delivery and questions',[
'Delivery items—**voice**, **stage presence**, **diction** and **team participation**—are worth 5 points each. Speak clearly, vary pace for emphasis, make eye contact with judges, gesture purposefully toward the poster and avoid fillers. Show enthusiasm—you are experts on your own research.',
'Be ready for judges’ questions about sample size, bias, ethics, surprising results or next steps. Answer honestly and concisely; if you do not know, say how you would find out.',
"Rehearse answers to possible questions as a team and decide who answers which type—methods, ethics, results or next steps—so responses are confident and organized.",
match('Likely judge question and strong answer focus',[['“How did you get consent?”','Describe your consent process and school permission'],['“Is your sample representative?”','Explain random sampling and limitations'],['“Does this prove causation?”','Explain association versus causation'],['“What surprised you?”','Share an unexpected finding'],['“What would you do next?”','Describe a follow-up study or intervention'],['“Why this topic?”','Connect to your community need']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Presentation day',{a:{text:'In rehearsal, your team reads every section of the poster aloud. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you change?',choices:[['Summarize key points and add context, pointing to the poster','b','Correct.',true],['Read faster to finish','b','Complement the poster instead of duplicating it.',false]]},b:{text:'A judge asks whether your study proves short sleep causes stress. How do you answer?',choices:[['Explain that it shows an association and describe how a future study could test causation','c','Correct.',true],['Say yes, it proves it','c','Avoid overstating.',false]]},c:{text:'Your presentation is engaging, accurate and complementary to the poster.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Teams stand beside the printed 48″ × 36″ poster for a 5-minute presentation (time card at one minute; notecards allowed); all members take an active role.','Begin with an enthusiastic elevator pitch (about 20–40 seconds) using a striking local finding; present the research story—problem, methods, key findings, implications and next steps—focusing on salient points.','Connect to the poster by pointing to charts and adding context without reading it; plan about 4:45 with equal parts; deliver with clear voice, eye contact and enthusiasm; answer questions honestly about consent, sampling, causation and next steps.'],terms:[['Elevator pitch','A short, enthusiastic introduction that captures interest.'],['Salient points','The most important findings and implications.'],['Complementary presentation','A talk that adds to the poster rather than repeating it.']],mcqs:[q('How should the presentation begin?','With an elevator pitch','By reading the title','With references','With acknowledgements','Requirement.'),q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','5 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','15 minutes','Time.'),q('How many points is the opening worth?','15','5','10','20','Rubric.'),q('Should the presentation duplicate the poster?','No—it should complement it','Yes, word for word','Only the abstract','Only the references','Requirement.'),q('Where do teams stand?','Next to their poster','In front of the poster','Behind the judges','Outside the room','Rule.'),q('Which question tests understanding of methods?','“Is your sample representative?”','“What is your favorite color?”','“Where did you print it?”','“What time is it?”','Methods.'),q('How many Round Two points depend on the three content items?','45','15','70','20','3 × 15.'),q('If you do not know an answer, you should:','Say so and explain how you would find out','Make something up','Ignore the question','Change the topic','Honesty.')],frq:{prompt:'Outline a five-minute Round Two presentation for your research poster that earns full content points.',rubric:[['Elevator pitch',['elevator pitch','hook','statistic','why it matters','enthusiastic']],['Research story',['question','methods','findings','implications','next steps','limitations']],['Poster connection',['point','charts','complement','not read','context']],['Structure and delivery',['5 minutes','4:45','each member','handoffs','eye contact','questions']]],model:'Our three-member team would stand beside our printed poster and open with an elevator pitch: “Two out of three students at our school sleep less than eight hours on school nights—and those sleeping the least were twice as likely to report high stress. We want to show you what we found and what our school can do about it.” Member 1 would then explain why we chose the question—our school nurse noticed many tired, stressed students—and state our research question. Member 2 would briefly describe our methods, pointing to the methods section: an anonymous, pilot-tested survey of 172 randomly selected students with informed consent, plus six interviews, and how we reduced bias. Member 2 would then present key findings, gesturing to our bar chart and explaining what surprised us, such as how often students named homework after midnight as the main barrier, while adding context not printed on the poster, like students’ reactions during the survey. Member 3 would explain our conclusions and implications—sleep education and a review of homework scheduling—along with limitations, such as self-reported data from one school, and next steps, such as testing a sleep-education lesson. Member 1 would close with a memorable line tying back to our title. We would rehearse to about 4 minutes 45 seconds, use notecards only, give each member an equal part and prepare honest answers about consent, sampling and why our study shows association rather than causation.'},sources:[RULES,STATS1]};
